    <item>
      <title>AI Bug Fixes, Crescent Island Power Brake &amp; Nouveau Fixes For Linux 7.3-rc5 DRM</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.3-rc5-DRM</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.3-rc5-DRM</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 26 Sep 2026 11:13:16 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="LINUX KERNEL" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/linuxkernel.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; The Direct Rendering Manager (DRM) subsystem fixes for the week are heavier on the side with many fixes to these kernel graphics/display drivers ahead of Sunday's Linux 7.3-rc5 release. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;DRM co-maintainer David Airlie noted that this week's pull is mostly made up of "&lt;em&gt;AI inspired fixes for error handling paths, leaks and use after frees&lt;/em&gt;" but some more notable fixes do stand out. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;For the Intel Xe driver with its ability to report reasons why the graphics card is throttling for the likes of memory or VR thermals, SoC average temperature, etc, there is now power brake as a new throttle reason with the upcoming Crescent Island AI accelerators. Crescent Island has a power break "PWRBRK" hardware bit as a new throttle reason that can be relayed by the Xe driver. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=nvidia-980-5080-linux&amp;image=nouveau_980_5_med" alt="NVIDIA graphics cards" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The Nouveau open-source NVIDIA driver has seen the most fixes with this week's DRM material for Linux 7.3. After Nouveau transitioned from using the NVIDIA R535 driver firmware binaries to the NVIDIA R570 firmware binaries, suspend/resume ended up regressing and other problems. This week's pull for Linux 7.3  has fixed suspend/resume when using the newer NVIDIA firmware, fixed vRAM pinning, and fixed various other driver issues -- some of which were discovered via AI/LLMs. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The full list of the DRM fixes now merged for Linux 7.3 can be found via &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/dri-devel/CAPM=9twjOfCE5-LzepSB58uaQtn4kS2yF2sOu52kiJp2xkJXkQ@mail.gmail.com/"&gt;this pull request&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Wisp Makes It Easier To Manage Btrfs Snapshots From The GNOME Desktop</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/GNOME-Wisp-Btrfs-Snapshots</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/GNOME-Wisp-Btrfs-Snapshots</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 26 Sep 2026 10:45:13 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="GNOME" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/gnome.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Wisp is a new GNOME Shell extension making it easier to graphically manage Btrfs file-system snapshots.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;What I found most interesting from the newest issue of &lt;a href="https://thisweek.gnome.org/posts/2026/09/twig-267/"&gt;This Week in GNOME&lt;/a&gt;, which was primarily about app updates following the recent GNOME 51 release, is Wisp as a new GNOME Shell extension. Wisp interfaces with Snapper to provide a nice means of managing Btrfs file-system snapshots on the system.  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=gnome_wisp_1" alt="GNOME Wisp" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;For those not wanting to interact with Snapper from the command line for managing their Btrfs-based Linux systems, Wisp provides an easy way to do so graphically within the modern GNOME desktop.  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=gnome_wisp_2" alt="GNOME Wisp Btrfs snapshot diff" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;From Wisp one can easily view what files have changed between given Btrfs snapshots, adjust retention policies and other Snapper related settings, manually create a new file-system snapshot, and related tasks.  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=gnome_wisp_3" alt="GNOME Wisp settings" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Wisp is available from &lt;a href="https://extensions.gnome.org/extension/10755/wisp/"&gt;extensions.gnome.org&lt;/a&gt; and can also be found via &lt;a href="https://github.com/epogonii/wisp"&gt;GitHub&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>AMDXDNA Linux Driver Being Enhanced For Current NPU3 Hardware</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMDXDNA-NPU3-Enhancements</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMDXDNA-NPU3-Enhancements</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 26 Sep 2026 10:33:18 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="AMD" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/amd.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; The AMDXDNA open-source Linux kernel accelerator driver is seeing a fair amount of work for better supporting current-generation NPU3 neural processing units. The AMD NPU3 is what's found in current AMD Ryzen AI 300 / Ryzen AI 300 PRO series SoCs. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;AMD engineer David Zhang has been working on the AMDXDNA driver to handle kernel-mode command submission for NPU3 hardware as well as improving system and run-time power management support like for suspend/resume. He explained in the latest revision of the patches: &lt;br /&gt;&lt;blockquote&gt;"This patch series extends the amdxdna accelerator driver to support AMD AIE4 (NPU3) platforms with kernel-mode command submission (KMQ), device telemetry queries, comprehensive system and runtime power management, and NPU3 classic device support. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Previously, AIE4 devices relied on userspace doorbell mapping for command dispatch. This series establishes kernel-mode submission as the default execution path, providing robust fence management, BO reservation locking, and queue lifecycle handling. Additionally, it implements full system suspend/resume and runtime power management across Physical Functions (PF), Virtual Functions (VF), and Classic devices, along with SR-IOV PM dependency linking and userspace runtime compatibility stubs."&lt;/blockquote&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The current set of 21 patches lay out the kernel-mode command submission, enabling new clock / frequency / information queries, suspend and resume support, and other improvements for AIE4/NPU3 hardware. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=2026&amp;image=ryzen_ai_npu_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=ryzen_ai_npu_med" alt="AMD Ryzen AI" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details for those interested in this pending AMDXDNA Linux driver work can find the latest patches on the &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20260926013448.3840921-1-yidong.zhang@amd.com/"&gt;LKML&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>KDE Plasma 6.8 Continues With Last Minute Tweaks &amp; Fixes</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Plasma-6.8-Last-Minute-UI-Fixes</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Plasma-6.8-Last-Minute-UI-Fixes</guid>
      <pubDate>Sat, 26 Sep 2026 09:56:52 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="KDE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/kde.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Even with this week's KDE Akademy conference and releasing &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/KDE-Plasma-6.8-Beta-2"&gt;KDE Plasma 6.8 Beta 2&lt;/a&gt;, KDE developers still found time to land last minute changes for Plasma 6.8 plus ongoing early work toward Plasma 6.9. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Plasma 6.8 saw some last minute UI adjustments, including the click feedback for the system tray icons being better than before. Plus smaller refinements in other areas for tweaking the UI to provide a better experience. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Plasma 6.8 also fixes a possible crash if deleting a starred clipboard entry from the Meta + V pop-up. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Plasma 6.8's Bluetooth setup wizard can also now find Bluetooth Low Energy devices while other software on the system is still holding open a discovery session for other devices, such as if using KDE Connect concurrently or other Bluetooth apps. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=kde_linux_dos_med" alt="KDE Plasma desktop" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;For Plasma 6.9, KRunner-powered searches now better handle currency conversions with the currency symbol being allowed on either side of the number. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details on this week's changes for the KDE Plasma desktop can be found via &lt;a href="https://blogs.kde.org/2026/09/26/this-week-in-plasma-akademy-special/"&gt;This Week in Plasma&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>New FUTEX Syscalls Back To Being Worked On For Helping Valve's ARM64 Gaming Ambitions</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/FUTEX-Robust-List2-Syscalls</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/FUTEX-Robust-List2-Syscalls</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 25 Sep 2026 22:42:16 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="VALVE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/valve.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Going back to last year the Igalia open-source consulting firm has been &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Igalia-Futex-ARM64-Linux-Work"&gt;working on enhancing the Linux kernel to help Steam Play gaming on ARM64&lt;/a&gt; and ensuring the FEX emulator is operating efficiently for x86/x86_64 games running on the likes of the Steam Frame. After other kernel work in recent months, there's finally an updated round of the FUTEX get_robust_list2 and set_robust_list2 system call patches. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Igalia has been working to address shortcomings of the existing get_robust_list2 / set_robust_list2 with these new versions to better align with the needs of FEX-Emu and other emulation layers. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2025&amp;image=fex_emu_syscall" alt="Igalia FEX slide" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;As the first update to the patches since late November, André Almeida of Igalia today posted a new version that is rebased against the latest mainline kernel, adapting to VDSO changes, and other code improvements. Updating these patches was held up by  addressing a race condition. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Those interested can find these new FUTEX set_robust_list2 / get_robust_list2 patches via &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20260925-tonyk-robust_futex-v7-0-d1095991152c@igalia.com/"&gt;this kernel patch series&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>GDB 18.1 Debugger Brings Better Windows Support</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/GNU-Debugger-GDB-18.1</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/GNU-Debugger-GDB-18.1</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 25 Sep 2026 16:47:52 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="GNU" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/gnu.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; GDB 18.1 is out today as the newest version of the GNU Debugger that works across C/C++, Fortran, Rust, and other languages like Go, Ada, and more. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Microsoft Windows is seeing a lot of love with the GDB 18.1 debugger release. GDB on Windows now has support for native Thread Local Storage (TLS) variables, the non-stop mode is supported on Windows 10 and later, scheduler locking now works, improved support for the Windows Terminal console, and other changes. On the user experience side, filenames are now consistently shown with forward slashes on Windows rather than a mix of backwards and forwards slashes. GDB 18.1 also added an AArch64 MinGW target. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;GDB 18.1 also improves the handling of inferior arguments, support for the Floating Point Mode Register (FPMR) on AArch64, and support for new commands like "save history", "set/show/unset local-environment", "info proc environ" and others. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;GDB's Python API has also continue to be improved as well as the debuggger's GAP protocol support. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;GDB 18.1 downloads and the full list of changes for this debugger feature release via &lt;a href="https://lists.gnu.org/archive/html/info-gnu/2026-09/msg00008.html"&gt;the GNU mailing list&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Redox OS Adds IO_uring-Like API, NUMA &amp; Gets QEMU Working</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/Redox-OS-August-2026</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/Redox-OS-August-2026</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 25 Sep 2026 15:51:38 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="OPERATING SYSTEMS" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/operatingsystems.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; The Redox OS open-source, Rust-based operating system has seen a lot of activity in recent weeks with many notable improvements landing. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Redox OS today published their latest monthly status update for highlighting all the interesting developments made over the course of August. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Redox OS in August implemented a ring buffer communication API akin to Linux's IO_uring. This can allow for better performance with supported drivers, such as for I/O with NVMe storage. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Redox OS has also implemented initial NUMA support as another nice improvement for the month. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The QEMU emulator is also now working on Redox but currently lacks KVM-like virtual machine acceleration. So QEMU works, but it will be very slow. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=redoxos_qemu" alt="RedoxOS QEMU usage" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Also notable is Redox OS now supporting dual boot installations from Linux. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;On the ARM64 front, Redox on AArch64 has initial multi-core CPU support. Some other Redox changes for the month include lowering IPC overhead by around 5%, decreasing the kernel binary size, and various driver improvements. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;More details on these recent improvements to Redox OS can be found via the &lt;a href="https://www.redox-os.org/news/this-month-260831/"&gt;Redox-OS.org blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Approaching A 10 Second Linux Kernel Build</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/review/near-10-sec-kernel-build</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/review/near-10-sec-kernel-build</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 25 Sep 2026 14:46:26 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt;     &lt;p&gt;The coffee window is closing. Going back many years with the time it's taken to compile the Linux kernel has been a well known opportunity for a coffee break or even eating a meal during the lengthy Linux kernel build process. Even when the Linux kernel was much smaller than it is today, with the hardware at the time it would often be a very time consuming process... Thus incredible to think that we are now on the horizon of a ten second clean, default kernel build on Linux x86_64.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=near-10-sec-kernel-build&amp;image=fast_linux_kernel_1_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=near-10-sec-kernel-build&amp;image=fast_linux_kernel_1_med" alt="Linux kernel coffee build" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Paired with very capable processors and the latest software innovations in the Linux kernel, building the latest Linux kernel on available hardware can be done in now roughly 15 seconds. Though with next-gen processors I am betting on 10 seconds. Phenomenal for being able to iterate faster from clean builds, if bisecting Linux kernel regressions, etc. The latest pending kernel build software improvements also help in proportionally reducing the kernel build times for those not on leading edge processors.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=near-10-sec-kernel-build&amp;image=fast_linux_kernel_2_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=near-10-sec-kernel-build&amp;image=fast_linux_kernel_2_med" alt="AMD EPYC 9575F" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Absolutely mind boggling to think compiling a defconfig x86_64 Linux kernel build in a matter of seconds -- especially for those that have been in the Linux kernel space for a while. Or for an allmodconfig build with every possible kernel module, now dropping under two minutes per build for significantly speeding up kernel build times there too. Big win for increased productivity and crazy to think about considering &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.3-rc1-Code-Stats"&gt;the Linux kernel source tree as a whole is nearly 41 million lines&lt;/a&gt;. The time it takes to compile the Linux kernel has been one of my favorite benchmarks over the &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Phoronix-Turns-22"&gt;22+ years running Phoronix&lt;/a&gt; and filled with excitement each time reviewing a new generation of CPUs to see the time consistently dropping even with the Linux kernel continuing to grow larger in step too.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;On the software side making such speedy builds possible moving forward has been a patch series by Linux MM developer Lorenzo Stoakes for optimizing kernel build times. Lorenzo with the assistance of AI/LLMs took to addressing many bottlenecks in the Kbuild code that were reducing the parallel potential of the kernel build process. I've been covering Lorenzo's progress over the past month that began with &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/AI-To-Faster-Linux-Kernel-Comp"&gt;AI Made A Lot Of "Hideous" Code But Found Major Bottlenecks For Faster Linux Compilation&lt;/a&gt; and has &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Faster-Kernel-Builds-AI-v2"&gt;continued being revised and looking like it may land for Linux 7.4&lt;/a&gt; with kernel builds ~36% faster and &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-Kbuild-Faster-v3"&gt;incremental kernel builds around 70% faster&lt;/a&gt;. Huge relative time savings and not being dependent upon just the newest or highest-end processors out there.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=faster_kbuild" alt="Lorenzo benchmarks of Kbuild improvement speed-up" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;Lorenzo this week posted his &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20260923-build-speedup-v4-0-73128809a4a4@kernel.org/"&gt;v4 patch series&lt;/a&gt; of these kbuild improvements. Currently at 22 patches with some patches being picked up so far in kbuild-for-next and there also being a few other patch series noted in that cover letter worth pulling in.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=near-10-sec-kernel-build&amp;image=fast_linux_kernel_3_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=near-10-sec-kernel-build&amp;image=fast_linux_kernel_3_med" alt="AMD EPYC CPUs" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;That patch series noted an AMD EPYC server with GCC having seen defconfig build times drop from 28 to 20 seconds. That was with a dual AMD EPYC 9754 server... The EPYC 9754 Bergamo is a nice Zen 4 processor but my current favorite CPU for code compilation workloads and many HPC tasks is the AMD EPYC 9575F.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=near-10-sec-kernel-build&amp;image=fast_linux_kernel_4_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=near-10-sec-kernel-build&amp;image=fast_linux_kernel_4_med" alt="AMD EPYC 9575F 2P server" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The EPYC 9575F with its 64 cores / 128 threads per socket allows for up to a 5.0GHz boost clock and all-core boost speed of 4.5GHz. Paired with 256MB of L3 cache, the AMD EPYC 9575F remains a very beautiful piece of silicon even two years after launch. (Coming up is the AMD EPYC 9686F high frequency Venice part that features 96 cores / 192 threads at up to 5.0GHz boost clock with 384MB of L3 cache... Paired with MRDIMM-12800 memory, I am already drooling for that one.)&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;At least until compilers achieve parallelizing compilation of individual source files, the very highest core counts aren't necessarily the best for build servers. In the past there has been &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/GCC-fparallel-jobs-Patches"&gt;some work on GCC parallelizing the compilation of individual files&lt;/a&gt; but years later nothing has crossed the finish line there.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=near-10-sec-kernel-build&amp;image=fast_linux_kernel_5_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=near-10-sec-kernel-build&amp;image=fast_linux_kernel_5_med" alt="Linux kernel build times for different CPUs" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;In &lt;a href="https://openbenchmarking.org/test/pts/build-linux-kernel-1.18.0#results"&gt;Linux kernel build time benchmarking&lt;/a&gt;, the AMD EPYC 9575F 2P consistently ranks the fastest setup for compiling the x86_64 defconfig kernel. Typically at around 20 seconds. That's already a great milestone considering and recalling the days when reviewing AMD EPYC Milan in finally breaking the 30 second mark for a kernel build.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;So with two AMD EPYC 9575F processors and on an Ubuntu 26.04 LTS setup, I decided to try out Lorenzo's v4 kernel patches as outlined in this week's LKML patch series.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class="content"&gt;   &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/linux-kernel-builds-amd-epyc-9575f-2p/result.svgz" alt="Linux Kernel Builds AMD EPYC 9575F 2P" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;The AMD EPYC 9575F 2P server setup was running with 24 x 64GB DDR5-6400 memory and a Samsung PM1743 3.84TB PCIe Gen 5.0 NVMe SSD. Stock Ubuntu 26.04 LTS setup otherwise. Running with EXT4 on the lone PCIe Gen5 NVMe SSD and not building the kernel off TMPFS in RAM or other aggressive setup in trying to push the kernel build times even higher than normal.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="//www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=near-10-sec-kernel-build&amp;image=fast_linux_kernel_6_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="//www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=near-10-sec-kernel-build&amp;image=fast_linux_kernel_6_med" alt="AMD EPYC power determinism" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;I did run the AMD EPYC 9575F processors both at their defaults and then in the AMD power determinism mode as one way to push for slightly better performance.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/linux-kernel-builds-amd-epyc-9575f-2p/timed-linux-kernel-compilation-defconfig.svgz" alt="Timed Linux Kernel Compilation benchmark with settings of Build: defconfig. v4-build-speedup + Power Determinism was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;A 15 second kernel build! A clean x86_64 Linux defconfig kernel in 15 seconds! Crazy thinking about how far Linux kernel builds have evolved in recent years with newer processors as well as the kernel build times reducing even with the Linux kernel continuing to grow larger, especially in the AI/LLM era. Lorenzo's patch series delivered a dramatic kernel build time improvement for the AMD EPYC 9575F 2P server dropping from 22 seconds to 15~17 seconds. The Kbuild improvements also deliver even more dramatic wins for relative performance for those doing more common incremental builds of the kernel when working on Linux kernel changes, bisecting kernel regressions, etc.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;div style="text-align: center;"&gt;&lt;img type="image/svg+xml" width="100%" height="auto" src="//phoronix.com/benchmark/result/linux-kernel-builds-amd-epyc-9575f-2p/timed-linux-kernel-compilation-allmodconfig.svgz" alt="Timed Linux Kernel Compilation benchmark with settings of Build: allmodconfig. v4-build-speedup + Power Determinism was the fastest." /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;  &lt;p&gt;With a Linux x86_64 allmodconfig for building all the Linux kernel modules relevant to x86_64, there was more than 30 seconds trimmed off the kernel build time with these pending patches. Doing allmodconfig builds on the Linux x86_64 kernel paired with the best high-end server processors is finally nearing the two minute mark.&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="//www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=near-10-sec-kernel-build&amp;image=fast_linux_kernel_7_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="//www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=near-10-sec-kernel-build&amp;image=fast_linux_kernel_7_med" alt="AMD EPYC 9575F processor" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;p&gt;These pending Kbuild patches are looking very promising for speeding up Linux kernel builds regardless of your CPU and whether performing clean or incremental builds. Now any bets if AMD's upcoming Venice CPUs like the recently announced EPYC 9686F + MRDIMMs + PCIe Gen6 storage will be able to cross the 10 second mark for a default Linux 7.4+ kernel build?&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Patches Posted For Enabling NVMe On All Apple M3 Based SoCs With Linux</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/NVMe-Apple-M3-Linux-Patches</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/NVMe-Apple-M3-Linux-Patches</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 25 Sep 2026 10:27:51 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="APPLE" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/apple.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; While &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.2-Features-Expected"&gt;Linux 7.2 brought initial Apple M3 support&lt;/a&gt; and Linux 7.3 brought &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/M3-Pro-Max-Ultra-Linux-7.3"&gt;M3 Pro / Max / Ultra support&lt;/a&gt;, the mainline Linux kernel support for the M3 series hardware remains very basic and not yet ready for daily driving. But more work continues to be upstreamed and today the patches were posted for getting NVMe storage up and running across all Apple M3 hardware.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Janne Grunau posted the seven patch series for enabling NVMe on all M3 and M3 Pro / Max / Ultra devices. The M3 series NVMe works the same way as with prior Apple M2 Pro / Max / Ultra devices. But because of Device Trees, another ~140 lines of YAML and DT additions are needed to get this NVMe support in place for the compatible Apple M3 series.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;Given the timing and this just being Device Tree additions for bringing up NVMe on the Apple M3 hardware, hopefully it will still make it in time for the Linux 7.4 merge window in October.  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/image-viewer.php?id=2026&amp;image=apple_m3_nvme_lrg" target="_blank"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=apple_m3_nvme_sml" alt="Apple + NVMe" /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;&lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/lkml/20260925-apple-m3-nvme-v1-0-5e832e2f4aa9@jannau.net/"&gt;The patches&lt;/a&gt; are out for review on the Linux kernel mailing list.  &lt;br /&gt;  &lt;br /&gt;While the Apple M3 support is still being laid out in the mainline kernel, those with Apple M3 hardware today wanting to run Linux are best off for now using &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Asahi-Linux-Official-M3"&gt;the latest downstream Asahi Linux with its Apple M3 support&lt;/a&gt; for carrying the latest work well before it reaches the official kernel tree.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>AMD Sends Out New Linux Patches Further Enhancing HDMI 2.1 Reliability</title>
      <author>Michael Larabel</author>
      <link>https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMDGPU-HDMI-2.1-Reliability</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.phoronix.com/news/AMDGPU-HDMI-2.1-Reliability</guid>
      <pubDate>Fri, 25 Sep 2026 10:12:09 +0000</pubDate>
      <description>&lt;div class="content"&gt; &lt;div style="float: left; padding: 0 10px 10px;"&gt;&lt;img alt="RADEON" src="https://www.phoronix.com/assets/categories/radeon.webp" width="100" height="100" /&gt;&lt;/div&gt; Linux 7.4 is going to be the big kernel release where &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/Linux-7.4-AMDGPU-HDMI-2.1-Go"&gt;the AMD graphics driver will finally expose HDMI 2.1 by default&lt;/a&gt; and comes with features including HDMI FreeSync, VRR, and ALLM. Ahead of that milestone there have been various other last minute &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/news/More-AMDGPU-For-Linux-7.4"&gt;tweaks and improvements to its HDMI 2.1 support&lt;/a&gt;. Sent out on Thursday was the latest round of AMDGPU DC patches that includes a few more patches for improving the HDMI 2.1 reliability. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The AMDGPU Display Core patches sent out for the week include improving HDMI 2.1 reliability. First up is a fix to skip HDMI FRL status polling when the link is down such as around hot-plugging actions to avoid the possibility of the display failing to light up. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;There is also a fix for the HDMI 2.1 LT timeout to better align with the HDMI specification to deliver a more consistent experience. The driver is also now serializing HDMI FRL status polling against link detect. Pretty minor changes but nice to see them working on HDMI 2.1 reliability ahead of the big Linux 7.4 merge window in October. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The DC patches this week also work on new DCN 4.2 and DCN 6.0 support with various adjustments being made there. Based on the patch timing and the like, it appears &lt;a href="https://www.phoronix.com/search/DCN+6"&gt;DCN 6&lt;/a&gt; will be coming with next-gen RDNA5 GPUs. &lt;br /&gt; &lt;br /&gt;The full list of these latest AMDGPU DC patches can be found on the &lt;a href="https://lists.freedesktop.org/archives/amd-gfx/2026-September/153798.html"&gt;mailing list&lt;/a&gt;. &lt;br /&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;&lt;img src="https://www.phoronix.net/image.php?id=2026&amp;image=hdmi_21_reliability" alt="HDMI 2.1 reliability" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;br /&gt;Separately, on Thursday was also the newest &lt;a href="https://lore.kernel.org/dri-devel/20260924174339.635232-1-alexander.deucher@amd.com/"&gt;AMDGPU and AMDKFD pull request&lt;/a&gt; to DRM-Next for Linux 7.4 material. There the pull was made up of mostly fixes with a lot of work going on around AMD GFX12.1 IP as well as the DCN 6 display block.&lt;/div&gt;</description>
    </item>
